docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:98:        <tr><td>Net current of a clipped affine density</td><td><strong>Refuted</strong></td><td>For <code>j=j0+g·(x-c)</code>, the independent identity <code>I=j0*A+g·Q1</code> agreed with the default cubic support path to <code>1.16e-15</code> of full-cell current.</td></tr>
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:99:        <tr><td>Moving-origin or current-centroid error</td><td><strong>Refuted</strong></td><td>The independent first moment <code>m=j0*Q1+Q2@g</code> agreed to <code>5.74e-16</code> of full-cell first-moment scale. Even the smallest numerically resolved slivers kept derived centroid error below <code>4.97e-7 m</code>; ordinary cuts were at round-off.</td></tr>
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:102:        <tr><td>Shared-node flux reconstruction moves a curved boundary</td><td><strong>Reproduced as coarse-order truncation, not a clip algebra error</strong></td><td>The ring is exact to <code>1.67e-16</code> on a general quadratic. On <code>q=((R²-R0²)²)/(8R0²)+Z²/2</code>, its quartic radial term produced <code>1.464e-3 Wb</code> maximum shared-node error on a 0.24 m ring, 1.011 times the exact six-vertex flux spread. The curved Solov'ev rung must measure convergence and induced intersection displacement.</td></tr>
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:130:    <p>Use fixed centroid <code>c=(R0,Z0)</code>, circumradius <code>a</code>, and vertices
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:134:    <code>A=integral dA</code>, <code>Q1=integral x dA=0</code>, and
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:135:    <code>Q2=integral x x^T dA</code>. The oracle is
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:136:    <code>I=j0*A+g·Q1</code> and <code>m1=j0*Q1+Q2@g</code>. Run at three radii and
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:157:    <code>q(R,Z)=((R²-R0²)²)/(8R0²)+Z²/2</code>. It obeys
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:158:    <code>DeltaStar(q)=R²/R0²+1</code>. Choose <code>R0=6.2 m</code> and set
docs/research/hex-clip-current-attribution.html:159:    <code>q_b=((R0+a)²-R0²)²/(8R0²)</code> with <code>a=0.8 m</code>; the exact LCFS is
